WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.550
Three agents answer the same caller.

2
00:00:02.870 --> 00:00:05.020
One is easier to inspect.

3
00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:07.365
One sounds more natural.

4
00:00:07.685 --> 00:00:10.085
One keeps listening while it speaks.

5
00:00:10.405 --> 00:00:12.955
Which one should book the AC repair?

6
00:00:13.275 --> 00:00:19.600
The cascaded design is explicit: speech-to-text, then a language model, then text-to-speech.

7
00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:23.520
It has more hops, so streaming and overlap matter.

8
00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:29.015
But it leaves a text checkpoint between what the caller said and what the agent will say.

9
00:00:29.335 --> 00:00:36.310
You can inspect it, filter it, swap one stage, or tune speech recognition for addresses and phone numbers.

10
00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:45.355
For a booking call, that modularity is valuable when the team needs to debug every decision or use different models for different stages.

11
00:00:45.675 --> 00:00:51.100
The cost is extra coordination and another place for latency to accumulate.

12
00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:56.495
Native speech-to-speech uses one model to hear audio and produce audio.

13
00:00:56.815 --> 00:01:02.090
It can preserve cues that a transcript loses, such as tone and hesitation.

14
00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:07.985
It can make responses feel more natural, and often reduces the number of model hops.

15
00:01:08.305 --> 00:01:10.755
The trade is inspectability.

16
00:01:11.075 --> 00:01:16.800
Audio arrives directly, so it is harder to filter the response before it is spoken.

17
00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:20.995
Audio tokens can also cost more than text tokens.

18
00:01:21.315 --> 00:01:34.740
As of October 2026, the repository describes current Realtime model prices and names as changing vendor facts; use them only as dated context, not as timeless architecture rules.

19
00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:37.485
Full duplex changes the rhythm.

20
00:01:37.805 --> 00:01:43.505
The live voice model can listen while speaking, while a backend handles reasoning and tools.

21
00:01:43.825 --> 00:01:54.650
The backend can send three kinds of updates: an instruction that changes direction, thinking that helps the model without being spoken, and commentary that can be said aloud.

22
00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:57.745
That last channel needs a hard boundary.

23
00:01:58.065 --> 00:02:00.665
“I’m checking Thursday” is progress.

24
00:02:00.985 --> 00:02:02.985
“You’re booked” is a claim.

25
00:02:03.305 --> 00:02:08.430
Commentary should contain the claim only after the backend has committed the booking.

26
00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:17.550
If the caller interrupts while the write is in flight, the application decides whether to finish or cancel it, then reports the actual result.

27
00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:20.745
Compare the choices across eight dimensions.

28
00:02:21.065 --> 00:02:25.440
Cascaded wins modularity, control, and inspectability.

29
00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:32.335
Native speech-to-speech tends to win naturalness and can reduce latency, but is harder to inspect.

30
00:02:32.655 --> 00:02:41.930
Full duplex can win overlapping conversation and interruption handling, while moving more design work into delegation and backend boundaries.

31
00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:46.225
Cost and debuggability depend on the actual workload.

32
00:02:46.545 --> 00:02:51.845
For a booking call, choose cascaded when inspection and stage-level control dominate.

33
00:02:52.165 --> 00:02:58.290
Choose native speech-to-speech when conversational timing matters and the tool boundary is strong.

34
00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:06.785
Choose full duplex when simultaneous listening and speaking are central, and the team can operate a backend delegation path.

35
00:03:07.105 --> 00:03:10.080
In every case, the control plane stays.

36
00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:14.425
The architecture changes where latency and uncertainty live.

37
00:03:14.745 --> 00:03:21.120
It does not change who owns authorization, grounded evidence, durable state, or evaluation.

38
00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:29.515
For the version-specific details and the questions to validate after joining, read `docs/gpt-live-1.md`.
